{"command": "cd /testbed && cat .specify/feature.json; echo ===; head -40 specs/001-transactions/spec.md; echo ===; grep -n \"R04\\|R05\\|R06\\|preview\\|bool\\|stale\" specs/001-transactions/spec.md | head -20", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "{\n  \"feature_directory\": \"specs/001-transactions\"\n}\n===\n# Feature Specification: Atomic Batch Transactions for TinyDB\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db), wrapping a supplied TinyDB instance without changing its ordinary APIs. Use the default table only. R01: apply(operations) accepts a list of operation dictionaries ({'op':'insert','document':dict} or {'op':'remove','doc_id':positive integer}); return a list of inserted document IDs (removes add no result); empty input returns []; unknown/malformed operations raise ValueError. R02: Batch application is atomic: validate and apply sequentially, but on any error restore the complete pre-batch default-table data; a remove targeting a missing ID is an error; previously acquired db.table('_default') handles must reflect both success and rollback. R03: Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents continue to work; inserted and returned nested data must not alias the caller's input; mutating an operation after apply cannot mutate the database.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Apply a batch of insert and remove operations (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer wraps an existing TinyDB database with the batch writer and submits a list of operations. Each operation is either an insert (carrying a document) or a remove (carrying a document ID). The operations are validated and applied in order to the default table, and the developer receives the IDs of the inserted documents, in submission order. Removes contribute no result.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core value of the feature; without it the feature does not exist.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create a database, apply a batch of inserts and removes, and assert on the returned IDs and the resulting default-table contents. This alone delivers a usable, if non-atomic, MVP.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty database, **When** `apply([{'op':'insert','document':{'a':1}}, {'op':'insert','document':{'b':2}}])` is called, **Then** it returns the two new document IDs in submission order and the default table contains both documents.\n2. **Given** a database containing a document with a known ID, **When** `apply([{'op':'remove','doc_id':<that ID>}])` is called, **Then** it returns `[]` and the document is no longer present.\n3. **Given** any database, **When** `apply([])` is called, **Then** it returns `[]` and the table is unchanged.\n4. **Given** a database, **When** a batch mixes inserts and removes, **Then** only inserted IDs are returned, in the order the inserts appeared.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Fail atomically and restore pre-batch state (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer submits a batch in which a later operation is malformed or targets a missing document. The whole batch must have no net effect: the default table is restored to exactly its pre-batch contents, including any documents inserted earlier in the same failed batch, and a `ValueError` is raised.\n\n**Why this priority**: Atomicity is the defining property of a transaction; without it the feature is unsafe to use.\n\n**Independent Test**: Pre-populate a database, apply a batch that fails partway (missing `doc_id` or malformed operation), and assert the table equals the pre-batch snapshot and that `ValueError` was raised.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a pre-existing document, **When** `apply([insert, remove of a missing ID])` is called, **Then** a `ValueError` is raised and the table contains only the pre-existing document (the earlier insert is rolled back).\n===\n"}